.TH UICTL 1 "2026-08-08" "uictl 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
uictl \- find, inspect, and drive running macOS GUI applications
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uictl
.I subcommand
[options]
.br
.B uictl
.RB [ \-h | \-H | \-\-help | \-\-HELP | \-? ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uictl
locates running applications, lists and activates their windows,
screenshots them (optionally with a numbered accessibility-element
overlay), walks their accessibility tree, clicks, types, sends key
combinations, waits for elements to appear, runs OCR, samples pixel
colors, and reads/writes the clipboard.
.PP
A small background daemon (auto-started on first use, listening on a Unix
domain socket at \fI~/.uictl/uictl.sock\fR) holds Accessibility / Screen
Recording permission grants and the element-id cache across invocations.
.PP
Every subcommand prints exactly one JSON object to standard output and
exits 0 on success, non-zero on failure. This output is intended to be
consumed by scripts or AI agents as readily as by a human.
.B uictl
can also run as an MCP server over stdio; see the
.B mcp
subcommand below.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B daemon \fR[start|stop|status]
Manage the background daemon. \fBstart\fR is invoked automatically by any
other command if the daemon isn't already running.
.TP
.B permissions \fR[--request]
Check, or trigger the OS prompts for, Accessibility and Screen Recording
permission.
.TP
.B apps \fR[--all]
List running applications.
.TP
.B windows \fR[--app <selector>]
List on-screen windows, optionally filtered to one application.
.TP
.B activate --app <selector> \fR[--window <id>]
Bring an application, and optionally a specific window, to the front.
.TP
.B screenshot \fR(--window <id> | --app <selector> | --screen <n>) [--out <path>] [--annotate] [--role <AXRole>]
Capture a window or display to a PNG file. With \fB--annotate\fR, overlays
a numbered box on every accessibility element and returns a JSON legend
mapping numbers to element ids, roles, titles, and frames.
.TP
.B elements \fR(--window <id> | --app <selector>) [--role <r>] [--title <substr>] [--max-depth <n>] [--max-elements <n>]
Walk a window's accessibility tree; returns each matching element's id,
role, title, value, and on-screen frame.
.TP
.B click \fR(--at <x,y> | --element <id>) [--button left|right|center] [--double] [--count <n>]
Click at a screen point or on the center of a specific element.
.TP
.B move --at <x,y>
Move the mouse cursor without clicking.
.TP
.B scroll --at <x,y> \fR[--dx <n>] [--dy <n>]
Scroll at a screen point.
.TP
.B type \fR<text> [--element <id>]
Type text into the focused control, or into a specific element (tries
setting its value directly first, falls back to focus + synthesized
keystrokes).
.TP
.B key \fR<combo>
Send a keyboard shortcut, e.g. \fIcmd+shift+4\fR or \fIctrl+alt+delete\fR.
.TP
.B wait\-for \fR(--window <id> | --app <selector>) [--role <r>] [--title <substr>] [--timeout <seconds>]
Block until a matching element appears, or the timeout elapses.
.TP
.B ocr \fR(--image <path> | --window <id> | --app <selector>) [--region <x,y,w,h>]
Recognize text in an image file or a captured window, optionally
restricted to a region.
.TP
.B pixel --at <x,y>
Sample the color of a single screen pixel.
.TP
.B clipboard \fR(get | set <text>)
Read or write the system clipboard.
.TP
.B mcp
Run as an MCP server over stdio, exposing every capability above as an MCP
tool.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-H ", " \-\-help ", " \-\-HELP ", " \-?
Show help. All five spellings are accepted at the top level and are
normalized onto ArgumentParser's native \fB\-\-help\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
List an application's windows, then screenshot it with element numbers:
.PP
.RS
.EX
uictl activate --app Safari
uictl screenshot --app Safari --annotate --out /tmp/safari.png
.EE
.RE
.PP
Click the element numbered 7 in that legend's \fIid\fR field, then confirm:
.PP
.RS
.EX
uictl click --element 12345-7
uictl elements --app Safari --title "Expected Label"
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.uictl/uictl.sock
Daemon IPC socket.
.TP
.I ~/.uictl/uictl.pid
Daemon process id.
.TP
.I ~/.uictl/daemon.log
Daemon stdout/stderr.
.TP
.I ~/.uictl/screenshots/
Default output location for \fBscreenshot\fR when \fB--out\fR is omitted.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success (the JSON response's \fIok\fR field is \fItrue\fR); non-zero
otherwise.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR screencapture (1),
.BR osascript (1)
